{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q002_mazg_hise_remote_homology","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08, both lineages retrieved in the same release): d1vmga_ (sunid 113676, a.204.1.2, 'MazG-like' family, described as hypothetical protein SSo12199/SSo3215 from Sulfolobus solfataricus) and d1y6xa1 (sunid 122676, a.204.1.4, 'HisE-like (PRA-PH)' family, described as phosphoribosyl-ATP pyrophosphatase HisE from Mycobacterium tuberculosis, chain 1y6x A:7-93) share class a (all alpha proteins), fold a.204 (all-alpha NTP pyrophosphatases) and superfamily a.204.1 (all-alpha NTP pyrophosphatases). They are in different families. Neither homology section has warnings or heterogeneity entries, and both report homology_implied_by_level true at the domain level. The MazG-like domain has annotations 'Structural genomics target' and 'complexed with li, unl', with no identified ligand; the HisE-like domain has no annotation comments. Neither domain has UniProt or Pfam cross-references in the response.

Interpretation: SCOPe supports a shared superfamily relationship, a.204.1, which indicates probable common ancestry at that level. Because the families differ, SCOPe does not assert a closer family-level relationship, so the two should not be described as members of the same family. The shared fold also means the two domains have the same overall all-alpha architecture as classified by SCOPe. This is a remote-homology relationship at superfamily depth, not a sequence-identity claim. I did not retrieve BLAST, FATCAT, or residue-alignment evidence for this pair, so I cannot state the extent of the alignment or the percent identity.

Limits and functional conclusions that go beyond the evidence: The superfamily label 'all-alpha NTP pyrophosphatases' is SCOPe's descriptive name for the grouping. It does not establish that the MazG-like domain hydrolyzes nucleotide triphosphates or has the phosphoribosyl-ATP pyrophosphatase activity of the HisE-like family. The MazG-like member's own record is only a hypothetical protein, so no catalytic function is recorded for it. It would be unsupported to conclude that the MazG-like domain participates in histidine biosynthesis, shares the HisE substrate, reaction, mechanism, catalytic residues or ligand site, or can substitute for HisE as a structural template. The unidentified 'li, unl' ligand on d1vmga_ cannot be used as evidence of a substrate. Establishing any of those claims would require retrieved alignment and residue-correspondence evidence, a structure with a bound ligand identified in the record, enzyme or functional assays, or a literature source that addresses the MazG-like domain's function. None of those were retrieved here. Also, the lineages are SCOPe classifications for the reference domains only and do not transfer to any external target.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1/homology","scop-homology/SKILL.md"]}]}
